  "query_preview": "model guidance has shown a shift in recent days to a more \nwestern phase to the dual upper lows with the northern stream system \nserving as the more dominant feature as it dives into the mid \nMississippi Valley by Wednesday night. With the southern stream low \nnow slower and further south along the Gulf coast...low level \nmoisture advection northward into the region will be delayed on \nWednesday with precipitation likely to hold off until later in the \nday at the earliest. The phasing of the energy will commence by \nThanksgiving Day as surface low pressure organizes over the Mid \nAtlantic and lifts north. Precipitation will become widespread \nacross much of the region by Thursday afternoon with a strong \neasterly low level jet pulling Atlantic moisture back west across \nthe Appalachians and into the eastern Midwest. Increasing winds on \nthe back side of the low will make for a raw...chilly and unpleasant \nafternoon and evening for Thanksgiving. There remains uncertainty \nwith respect to the timing and location of the upper level energy \nphasing but the trend has been to shift to a wetter Thursday.\n\nPrecip type should largely be in the form of rain although low level \nthermals do support the potential for light snow to mix in at \ntimes...primarily Thursday evening into early Friday. The \ncombination of marginal boundary layer temperatures and warm ground \ntemps will keep any snow from largely being a factor although lows \nThursday night in the upper 20s and lower 30s could cause some slick \nspots if residual moisture is able to freeze despite the still \nrelatively warm ground.\n\nThe low pressure will kick out into New England and the Canadian \nMaritimes late week with precipitation ending over the forecast area \nearly Friday. An upper level trough will settle across the eastern \nhalf of the country over the weekend...maintaining periodic clouds \nand the potential for a few light showers as weak disturbances aloft \npass through the region. Daytime temperatures will remain in the \n40s, perhaps breaching back into the low 50s by the weekend.",
